{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in storytelling, direction, and performances, weaving together the origins of facebook with the tension of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the film doesn’t just chronicle the birth of a social media giant—it dissects the emotional and legal fallout that accompanied its rise. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and chilling emotional detachment with uncanny nuance. his portrayal isn’t a caricature of genius; it’s a portrait of someone who sees people as variables in a system, not as individuals.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a triumph—fast-paced, densely layered, and laced with wit and intellectual firepower. every line feels deliberate, every exchange a battle of wits, whether in the courtroom or the dorm room. sorkin’s dialogue crackles with energy, turning legal depositions into gripping drama and turning code into poetry. the film’s structure, alternating between two lawsuits and the timeline of facebook’s creation, never feels confusing; instead, it builds a mosaic of truth, perception, and motive.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally exceptional. jesse plemons as justin timberlake’s portrayal of sean parker is electric—a charismatic, chaotic force who embodies the wild, unregulated spirit of the early internet. andrew garfield brings heart and vulnerability to eduardo saverin, whose betrayal becomes the film’s emotional core. the chemistry between the actors is electric, making their fractures feel devastatingly real.\n\ndante spinotti’s cinematography is cool and clinical, mirroring the digital world the characters inhabit, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is haunting, minimalist, and perfectly suited to the film’s mood—a pulsing, electronic heartbeat that underscores the loneliness beneath the connectivity. the editing by jeffrey ford is flawless, pacing the narrative with surgical efficiency.\n\nbeneath its sleek surface, the social network is a tragedy. it’s about how technology can isolate even as it connects, how ambition can hollow out relationships, and how the pursuit of greatness often leaves behind a trail of broken friendships. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg—it interrogates him. and in doing so, it holds up a mirror to a generation obsessed with validation, connection, and control.\n\nthis isn’t just a movie about facebook. it’s a movie about the modern condition: how we build empires on the backs of others, how we mistake popularity for purpose, and how the most powerful inventions often come with the heaviest human costs. the social network is intelligent, gripping, and unforgettable—a defining film of the 21st century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.371579885482788}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " storytelling", "logprob": -0.7669656872749329}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -3.218599158572033e-05}, {"token": " direction", "logprob": -0.056976623833179474}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " performances", "logprob": -1.2377150058746338}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.2019551694393158}, {"token": " weaving", "logprob": -1.467298984527588}, {"token": " together", "logprob": -0.14443044364452362}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014659079723060131}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -1.86930513381958}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.7418